---
import BlogPost from '../../layouts/BlogPost.astro';
import { withUtm, track } from '../../lib/links';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const listingSlug = 'inactive-user-hygiene';
const listing = withUtm('https://marketplace.atlassian.com/apps/2720285106/inactive-user-hygiene', listingSlug, 'blog-post');
const listingTrack = track('atlassian', listingSlug, 'blog-post');
const rows = [
  ['User Management for Jira (bulk and timed inactive users)', 'TechTime Initiative Group', '4.8 (25 reviews)', 'USD 1,000', 'Bulk and scheduled deactivation from Jira, also for Data Center', 'https://marketplace.atlassian.com/apps/1213211/user-management-for-jira'],
  ['User Management for Jira and Auto-deactivation', 'miniOrange', '5.0 (8 reviews)', 'USD 850', 'Scheduler that deactivates inactive users automatically', 'https://marketplace.atlassian.com/search?query=User%20Management%20for%20Jira%20%26%20Auto-deactivation'],
  ['Flexible User License', 'OSCi', '4.8 (22 reviews)', 'USD 1,000', 'Automatically excludes inactive users from licensed groups', 'https://marketplace.atlassian.com/search?query=Flexible%20User%20License'],
  ['User/Group Export and License Optimizer', 'Aety admin apps', '4.9 (18 reviews)', 'USD 1,000', 'User and group export with license optimisation views', 'https://marketplace.atlassian.com/search?query=User%2FGroup%20Export%20%26%20License%20Optimizer'],
  ['User Management and License Optimizer', 'resolution', '4.4 (9 reviews)', 'USD 1,000', 'Find and deactivate inactive users across Jira and Confluence', 'https://marketplace.atlassian.com/search?query=User%20Management%20and%20License%20Optimizer'],
  ['Inactive-User Hygiene', 'Katabarwa Labs', 'new listing', 'USD 1,500', 'Read-only idle worklist from Jira activity; opt-in removal; Runs on Atlassian', 'https://marketplace.atlassian.com/apps/2720285106/inactive-user-hygiene'],
];
---
<BlogPost
  title="Jira inactive user apps compared: six Marketplace options for reclaiming licenses"
  description="An honest comparison of the Atlassian Marketplace apps that find inactive Jira users and reclaim licenses: what each measures, whether it deactivates for you, what it needs, and list price at 100 users, with the native route and where Inactive-User Hygiene fits."
  tag="Jira administration"
  pubDate="September 10, 2026"
  readMins={7}
  image="/apps/jira-inactive-user-hygiene/h1.png"
  app="jira-inactive-user-hygiene"
>
  <p>
    Disclosure first: we build one of the apps below, Inactive-User Hygiene, so read this as a
    vendor's comparison and check the listings yourself. Every fact here comes from the public
    Marketplace listing pages as of September 2026, and the price column is the listed annual price
    at the 100-user tier, which is where most instances land. Prices change; the listing is the
    source of truth.
  </p>

  <h2>What the native route gives you</h2>
  <p>
    Before any app: the org directory export at admin.atlassian.com has a "last seen" column, and
    deactivating an account there is the only action that frees a license seat. That is worth doing
    once regardless of what you buy. Its limits are that "last seen" is org-wide rather than
    Jira-specific, and it is a manual sweep. Atlassian's own docs:
    <a class="text-brand" href="https://support.atlassian.com/user-management/docs/deactivate-a-managed-account/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">deactivate a managed account</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The apps, side by side</h2>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full text-[14.5px]">
      <thead><tr class="border-b border-line text-left text-muted"><th class="py-2 pr-3">App</th><th class="py-2 pr-3">Vendor</th><th class="py-2 pr-3">Rating</th><th class="py-2 pr-3">List price, 100 users, per year</th><th class="py-2">What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map((r) => (
          <tr class="border-b border-line align-top">
            <td class="py-2 pr-3 font-semibold"><a class="text-brand" href={r[5]} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{r[0]}</a></td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-3">{r[1]}</td><td class="py-2 pr-3">{r[2]}</td><td class="py-2 pr-3">{r[3]}</td><td class="py-2">{r[4]}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>The questions that actually separate them</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>What counts as inactive?</strong> Most apps use last login. Inactive-User Hygiene uses
      last Jira activity from issue history instead, because a user who logs in for Confluence still
      holds an idle Jira seat. Neither is wrong; know which one you are measuring.</li>
    <li><strong>Does it deactivate for you?</strong> TechTime, miniOrange, OSCi, and resolution all
      automate deactivation or license-group removal on a schedule. Inactive-User Hygiene is
      report-first: the worklist is the product, and the only write is an opt-in, previewed removal
      of the users you confirm. If you want hands-off automation, pick one of the first group.</li>
    <li><strong>Where does the data go?</strong> Inactive-User Hygiene is a Forge app with no external
      egress, eligible for Atlassian's Runs on Atlassian marker. Check each other listing's Privacy
      and Security tab for where it processes user data; several run on Connect with a vendor backend.</li>
    <li><strong>Data Center too?</strong> TechTime and resolution cover Data Center. Ours is Cloud only.</li>
    <li><strong>Price at scale.</strong> Tiers diverge sharply above 500 users. Run your exact user
      count through each listing's calculator before comparing anything else.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where Inactive-User Hygiene fits</h2>
  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Our tool:</strong> <a class="text-brand font-semibold" href={listing} {...listingTrack} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Inactive-User Hygiene</a>
    on the Atlassian Marketplace, and the <a class="text-brand font-semibold" href={`${base}/atlassian/jira-inactive-user-hygiene`}>product page</a>.
  </div>
  <p>
    Choose it when you want a defensible list before anyone touches an account, measured on Jira
    activity rather than login, with the data never leaving your tenant. Choose one of the
    automation-first apps when your policy is settled and you want deactivation to just happen.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-6">
    Related reading:
    <a class="text-brand" href={`${base}/blog/find-inactive-jira-users-reclaim-licenses`}>How to find inactive Jira users and reclaim their licenses</a>.
  </p>
</BlogPost>
